'''Upper Chehalis''' ({{IPAc-en|ʃ|ə|ˈ|h|eɪ|l|ɪ|s|audio=en-us-Chehalis.oga}} {{Respell|shə|HAY|liss}}; {{langx|cjh|q̓ʷay̓áyiɬq̓|label=Upper Chehalis}}){{Sfn|Kinkade|1991|p=v}} is a [[Tsamosan languages|Tsamosan language]] historically spoken by the [[Satsop people|Satsop]] and [[Upper Chehalis people]] in western [[Washington (state)|Washington state]].

== Classification ==
Upper Chehalis is within the [[Tsamosan languages|Tsamosan branch]] of the [[Salishan languages|Salishan language family]].{{Sfn|Thompson|1979|p=693}}{{Sfn|Thompson|Kinkade|1990|p=35}} Within the Tsamosan languages, Upper Chehalis is within the Inland branch, alongside [[Cowlitz language|Cowlitz]]. Despite its name, it is more closely related to Cowlitz than [[Lower Chehalis language|Lower Chehalis]], which is within the Maritime branch alongside [[Quinault language|Quinault]].{{Sfn|Thompson|1979|p=693}} 

The [[Proto-Salish language]] likely originated north on the [[Salish Sea]], near the mouth of the Fraser River. The ancestors of the speakers of the Tsamosan branch likely branched off around the same time as the Interior Salishan languages branched off, settling south of [[Puget Sound]], eventually occupying as far as the Pacific Coast. These speakers were the ancestors of the Tsamosan languages today. Before they reached the coast, another branch headed further south and became the ancestors of the [[Tillamook language]] on the [[Oregon Coast]].{{Sfn|Thompson|1979|p=695}}

== Dialects ==
Upper Chehalis has three dialects: Satsop, Oakville Chehalis, and Tenino Chehalis. Because of the lack of data, the exact boundaries of these dialects cannot be determined. Kinkade supposed that Tenino was spoken upriver of [[Grand Mound, Washington|Grand Mound]], and that of the five Upper Chehalis bands, the {{Lang|cjh|sɬačáw̓am̓š}} and {{Lang|cjh|sq̓ʷay̓áyiɬq̓}} spoke Oakville Chehalis, while the {{Lang|cjh|ɬmə́šluws|italic=yes}}, {{Lang|cjh|ʔílawiqs}}, and {{Lang|cjh|c̓ax̣ʷásn̓}} spoke Tenino Chehalis. Kinkade himself coined the terms "Oakville Chehalis" and "Tenino Chehalis"; previously they had been called "Upper Chehalis 1" and "Upper Chehalis 2" by [[Franz Boas]]. The Tenino dialect had also been referred to as "Staktamish".{{Sfn|Kinkade|1991|p=v}}

The distinguishing feature between Tenino Chehalis and Oakville Chehalis is the phonemes [{{IPA|tʃ}}] [{{IPA|tʃʼ}}] and [{{IPA|ʃ}}] in Oakville and [{{IPA|k}}] [{{IPA|kʼ}}] and [{{IPA|x}}] in Tenino. The Tenino dialect is thus connected to the neighboring Cowlitz language, which also uses [{{IPA|k}}] [{{IPA|kʼ}}] and [{{IPA|x}}].{{Sfn|Kinkade|1991|p=v}} 

The Satsop dialect was originally spoken by the [[Satsop people]]. Although they spoke Upper Chehalis, they were more closely affiliated with the [[Lower Chehalis people|Lower Chehalis]] groups downriver. For this reason, Satsop contains many vocabulary items from the Lower Chehalis language.{{Sfn|Kinkade|1991|p=v}}

== History ==
The language was originally spoken on the [[Chehalis River (Washington)|Chehalis River]] in southwestern Washington, from around [[Elma, Washington|Elma]] upriver to [[Rainbow Falls State Park|Rainbow Falls]].  In Upper Chehalis, the language and people are called {{Lang|cjh|q̓ʷay̓áyiɬq̓}}, derived from the name for Mud Bay, {{Lang|cjh|q̓ʷayáiɬ}}, suggesting that  it was probably at one point also spoken along [[Mud Bay, Thurston County, Washington|Mud Bay]] on Puget Sound.{{Sfn|Kinkade|1991|p=v, 118}} The language was spoken by the [[Satsop people|Satsop]] on the [[Satsop River]] and the five aboriginal bands of Upper Chehalis on the Chehalis River: the {{Lang|cjh|sq̓ʷay̓áyiɬq̓}} on the [[Black River (Chehalis River tributary)|Black River]] and Mud Bay, the {{Lang|cjh|sɬačáw̓am̓š}} around [[Oakville, Washington|Oakville]], the {{Lang|cjh|ɬmə́šluws|italic=yes}} around [[Tenino, Washington|Tenino]], the {{Lang|cjh|ʔílawiqs}} around [[Chehalis, Washington|Chehalis]], and the {{Lang|cjh|c̓ax̣ʷásn̓}} around [[Pe Ell, Washington|Pe-Ell]] and [[Boistfort, Washington|Boistfort]].{{Sfn|Kinkade|1991|p=v}} 

By the 1970s, the language was [[Moribund language|moribund]] with only one competent speaker remaining.{{Sfn|Kinkade|1991|p=v}} In 1991, an Upper Chehalis dictionary was published by linguist [[M. Dale Kinkade]]. Most of the material in the language was compiled from two primary sources in the 1960s and 1970s: Silas Heck and Lillian Young, two native speakers living on the Chehalis Reservation. Other secondary sources were used, including other Chehalis people living on the reservation and in nearby communities who knew some of the language, as well as tapes made in the 1950s by [[Leon Metcalf]] and other collected notes on vocabulary from a variety of sources.{{Sfn|Kinkade|1991|p=v-vi}} 

